  "enhanced_information": "The clinical presentation of the 13-year-old girl, including fever, headache, photophobia, nausea, neck rigidity, and a maculopapular rash, along with cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) analysis showing elevated white cell count with lymphocytic predominance, suggests viral meningitis. The clear appearance of the CSF and normal glucose levels further support a viral etiology. Echovirus is a common cause of viral meningitis in children, particularly following exposure to environments like summer camps, where viral infections can spread easily. Herpes simplex virus, while a potential cause, is less common in this age group for viral meningitis. Bacterial causes such as Neisseria meningitidis and Streptococcus pneumoniae are unlikely due to the CSF findings of lymphocytic predominance and low neutrophil count. Therefore, the most likely causal organism is Echovirus."
